<span class="kicker">2026-07-23 / RIDGEWELL GUIDES</span><h1>How to Build a Paver Walkway: A Complete DIY and Hiring Guide</h1>
</div>
</section>
<section class="section article-section"><div class="wrap editorial-layout"><article class="prose blog-content"><p>A paver walkway is one of the simplest ways to add curb appeal and function to a yard. It guides foot traffic, ties your landscape together, and holds up for years when it is built correctly. The problem is that “built correctly” is doing a lot of work in that sentence. A paver walkway that looks easy on a weekend DIY video can shift, sink, or crack within a season if the base underneath it is not done right.</p>
<p>At Ridgewell Landscape and Design, we have installed and repaired hundreds of paver walkways and<a href="/blog/how-to-build-a-paver-patio/"> paver patios</a> across the Denver area, including Broomfield, Parker, and the surrounding communities. This guide walks through exactly how a paver walkway is built, step by step, so you can decide whether this is a project to tackle yourself or one worth handing to a professional.</p>
<h2><b>Should You DIY or Hire a Professional?</b></h2>
<p>Both paths can work. It really comes down to your timeline, tools, and how much risk you are willing to take on if something goes wrong underground.</p>
<table><tbody><tr><td><b>Factor</b></td><td><b>DIY</b></td><td><b>Hiring a Professional</b></td></tr><tr><td>Upfront cost</td><td>Lower, mainly materials</td><td>Higher, includes labor and equipment</td></tr><tr><td>Time investment</td><td>A full weekend or more for a short walkway</td><td>Usually completed in one to two days</td></tr><tr><td>Tools required</td><td>Rent a plate compactor, need a level, rubber mallet</td><td>Contractor brings all equipment</td></tr><tr><td>Risk of settling or shifting</td><td>Higher if base prep is rushed</td><td>Lower, base is engineered for your soil and climate</td></tr><tr><td>Warranty</td><td>None</td><td>Most reputable companies offer a workmanship guarantee</td></tr><tr><td>Best for</td><td>Short, straight walkways with simple grading</td><td>Longer walkways, slopes, drainage issues, or tie ins to patios</td></tr></tbody></table><p>If your walkway is short, flat, and straight, a motivated homeowner with the right tools can absolutely build it themselves. If you are dealing with slopes, poor drainage, tree roots, or a longer path, the margin for error shrinks fast, and that is usually when it makes sense to bring in a professional.</p>
<h2><b>What to Know Before You Start</b></h2>
<p>A little planning up front saves a lot of frustration later. Before you touch a shovel, work through these basics.</p>
<ul><li>Call 811 to have utility lines marked before you dig. This is free and prevents accidentally hitting a gas or utility line.</li><li>Check your local permit requirements. Many walkways do not need a permit, but it is worth a quick call to your city.</li><li>Plan for drainage. Water should slope away from your home and any structures, not pool along the path.</li><li>Measure twice. Know your total square footage before ordering pavers so you are not short mid project.</li><li>Think about how the walkway connects to existing hardscape, like a patio, driveway, or front step.</li></ul>
<h2><b>Tools and Materials You Will Need</b></h2>
<p><b>Materials</b></p>
<ul><li>Pavers of your choice, budget for 5 to 10 percent extra for cuts and waste</li><li>Crushed gravel or road base, typically 4 to 6 inches deep</li><li>Landscape fabric</li><li>Coarse bedding sand</li><li>Polymeric sand for joints</li><li>Paver edge restraints and spikes</li></ul>
<p><b>Tools</b></p>
<ul><li>Shovel and spade</li><li>Wheelbarrow</li><li>A<a href="/blog/choose-the-right-plate-compactor-size-for-a-patio-or-driveway/"> properly sized plate compactor</a>, rentable from most hardware stores</li><li>Level, at least a 4 foot level</li><li>Rubber mallet</li><li>String line and stakes</li><li>Paver saw or angle grinder with a masonry blade</li><li>Push broom</li></ul>

