<h2><b>Step by Step: How to Build a Paver Walkway</b></h2>
<h3><b>Step 1: Plan and Mark Your Layout</b></h3>
<p>Use stakes and string, or a garden hose, to mark the exact path and width of your walkway. Most walkways range from 3 to 4 feet wide, but this depends on how the path will be used. Stand back and check the layout from a few angles before committing to it.</p>
<h3><b>Step 2: Excavate the Path</b></h3>
<p>Dig out the marked area to a depth that accounts for your base, sand, and paver thickness combined. As a general rule, plan for 7 to 9 inches total depth. Keep the sides of the excavation straight and remove any roots, rocks, or debris.</p>
<h3><b>Step 3: Add and Compact the Base</b></h3>
<p>Fill the excavated area with 4 to 6 inches of crushed gravel or road base. Add it in layers of about 2 inches, compacting each layer with a plate compactor before adding the next. This step matters more than any other. A poorly compacted base is the number one reason paver walkways shift and sink over time. For a closer look at getting this right, see our<a href="/blog/paver-base-depth-for-patios-proper-installation-tips/"> guide to proper paver base depth</a>.</p>
<h3><b>Step 4: Lay Landscape Fabric</b></h3>
<p>Roll landscape fabric over the compacted base. This keeps soil and weeds from working their way up through the joints later, without blocking proper drainage.</p>
<h3><b>Step 5: Add and Screed the Bedding Sand</b></h3>
<p>Spread about 1 inch of coarse sand over the fabric. Use screed rails, which can be simple pipes or boards, to drag a straight edge across the sand and create a level, even surface for the pavers to sit on.</p>
<h3><b>Step 6: Lay the Pavers</b></h3>
<p>Starting from one end, place pavers snugly against each other following your chosen pattern. Use a rubber mallet to tap each paver gently into the sand bed. Check your level regularly as you go, since small errors compound quickly over a long walkway.</p>
<h3><b>Step 7: Cut Pavers to Fit</b></h3>
<p>At the edges and any curves, you will need to cut pavers to fit the space. A paver saw gives the cleanest cuts, though an angle grinder with a masonry blade works for smaller jobs. Always wear eye protection for this step.</p>
<h3><b>Step 8: Install Edge Restraints</b></h3>
<p>Once all pavers are placed, install edge restraints along both sides of the walkway and secure them with spikes. This keeps the entire installation from spreading apart over time, especially through freeze and thaw cycles.</p>
<h3><b>Step 9: Fill the Joints with Polymeric Sand</b></h3>
<p>Spread polymeric sand over the finished walkway and sweep it into the joints between pavers. Remove excess sand from the surface, then lightly mist the area with water to activate the polymers, which harden the sand in place and help resist weeds and ants.</p>
<h3><b>Step 10: Final Compaction and Cleanup</b></h3>
<p>Run the plate compactor over the finished walkway one more time to set everything firmly in place. Sweep off any remaining sand and inspect the surface for any pavers that sit higher or lower than their neighbors, adjusting as needed.</p>
<h2><b>Common Mistakes That Lead to a Failing Walkway</b></h2>
<ul><li>Skipping proper compaction of the base layer</li><li>Using too little base material, especially in areas with heavy clay soil</li><li>Ignoring drainage, which leads to water pooling and erosion under the pavers</li><li>Ordering pavers without accounting for cuts, then mixing in slightly different batches</li><li>Rushing the leveling process, which causes uneven, tripping-hazard surfaces over time</li></ul>
<h2><b>Why Colorado’s Climate Adds a Layer of Difficulty</b></h2>
<p>Front Range weather is tough on hardscaping. Freeze and thaw cycles cause the ground to expand and contract repeatedly through the winter and spring, a process known as frost heave. If the base beneath a walkway is not deep enough or properly compacted, this movement will eventually push pavers up or out of alignment. This is one of the main reasons base depth and compaction matter even more here than in milder climates, and it is something we account for on every project we build in the Denver area.</p>
<h2><b>How Much Does a Paver Walkway Cost</b></h2>
<table><tbody><tr><td><b>Approach</b></td><td><b>Typical Cost Range</b></td><td><b>What’s Included</b></td></tr><tr><td>DIY</td><td>8 to 15 dollars per square foot</td><td>Materials only, tool rental extra</td></tr><tr><td>Professionally installed</td><td>18 to 35 dollars per square foot</td><td>Materials, labor, equipment, base engineering, warranty</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Costs vary based on paver material, walkway length, site conditions, and any grading or drainage work needed. A short, simple DIY walkway can cost a few hundred dollars in materials. A longer or more complex professionally installed walkway, especially one with curves, borders, or a tie in to a patio, will land at the higher end of the range. For a broader breakdown of pricing across different hardscape projects, see our<a href="/blog/how-much-does-hardscaping-cost/"> hardscaping cost guide</a>.</p>
<h2><b>When It Is Worth Hiring a Professional</b></h2>
<p>DIY makes sense for small, straightforward projects. It is worth bringing in a professional when:</p>
<ul><li>The walkway is long, curved, or connects multiple areas of your yard</li><li>Your yard has a noticeable slope or drainage challenges</li><li>You want a guaranteed, long-term result backed by a workmanship warranty</li><li>You are combining the walkway with a larger project, like a patio, retaining wall, or outdoor kitchen</li><li>You would rather spend the weekend enjoying your yard than building it</li></ul>
<p>With over 15 years of building custom hardscapes across the Denver area, our team at Ridgewell Landscape and Design has seen firsthand how the right base work and material choices make the difference between a walkway that lasts a season and one that lasts decades. Explore our<a href="/services/paver-patios/"> paver patio services</a> to see the kind of work we build to last.</p>

<h2><b>Frequently Asked Questions</b></h2>
<h3><b>How long does it take to build a paver walkway?</b></h3>
<p>A short DIY walkway can take a full weekend, including base prep and paver laying. Professionally installed walkways are typically completed in one to two days, depending on length and site conditions.</p>
<h3><b>Do I need a permit to build a paver walkway?</b></h3>
<p>Most residential walkways do not require a permit, but requirements vary by city. It is worth a quick call to your local building department to confirm before starting.</p>
<h3><b>What is the best base depth for a paver walkway in Colorado?</b></h3>
<p>A base depth of 4 to 6 inches of compacted gravel is standard, though areas with heavy clay soil or significant frost heave may need a deeper base for long-term stability.</p>
<h3><b>Can I build a paver walkway over an existing concrete path?</b></h3>
<p>It is possible in some cases, but it depends on the condition and slope of the existing concrete. A professional assessment is the safest way to confirm whether this will hold up long term.</p>
<h3><b>How do I stop weeds from growing between my pavers?</b></h3>
<p>Landscape fabric under the base, combined with polymeric sand in the joints, is the most effective combination for keeping weeds from establishing between pavers.</p>
<h3><b>Is polymeric sand really necessary?</b></h3>
<p>It is not strictly required, but it significantly improves joint stability, reduces weed growth, and helps deter ants and other insects compared to regular sand.</p>
